<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#umm-al-walad">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Umm al-walad</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Umm al-walad, Arabic for 'mother of the child', was the title given in the Islamic world to an enslaved concubine who had borne a child that her master acknowledged as his. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> Umar forbade owners to sell or give away such women and had them freed at their master's death, a rule all the prominent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> legal schools adopted; Ali later declared that they could still be sold, a view that passed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiism">Shi'ism</a>. Their children by the master were born free and legitimate. From Yazid III in 744 the last Umayyad caliphs and most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> were sons of slave mothers, and dynasties such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> routinely had their heirs by concubines until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> was abolished in the 20th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/umm-al-walad.jpg" alt="Umm al-walad" width="330" height="385" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous / Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Khourrem.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In the time of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a> such women were regarded as property and could be sold by their owners. Umar's policy as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, forbidding their sale or gift and freeing them at the owner's death, was kept by Uthman, and Ali, Muhammad's cousin and son-in-law, at first agreed with it. Only in the later part of his own caliphate did Ali reverse it. His view entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiism">Shi'ism</a> together with the acceptance of temporary marriage, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> schools of law held to Umar's rule.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Islamic law allowed a Muslim man sexual relations with his female slave unless she was legally owned by his wife. Acknowledging paternity was optional. The child of a slave was born a slave unless the owner acknowledged the child as his, in which case it was free by law and often treated much like the children of his free wives. An unmarried slave who bore a child that her owner did not acknowledge faced charges of zina.</p><p>Many Muslim rulers chose to father heirs with the concubines of their harems rather than with free wives, because a wife's family clan could gain influence through the marriage, while a slave concubine had no family to interfere. The Ottoman sultans rarely married at all after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fall-of-constantinople">conquest of Constantinople</a> in 1453.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Zayd ibn Ali's failed bid for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> in 740 marked a turning point in favour of leaders born to slave mothers. The rise of such caliphs drew rhetoric on both sides. Yazid III declared that his mother was a Persian princess of the Sassanid dynasty and pointed to his descent from both Caesar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">Khagan</a>, while opponents of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marwan-ii">Marwan II</a> claimed his slave mother was already pregnant when she was captured, so that he was not truly the son of Muhammad ibn Marwan. The practice lasted as long as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in the Muslim world: Baraka Al Yamaniyah, a concubine of King Abdulaziz of Saudi Arabia, was the mother of Muqrin bin Abdulaziz, crown prince in 2015.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Umm al-walad” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
